# Types
Don't use `any` or `as unknown as` unless absolutely necessary. We want the type system to help us catch errors and we have to use good types for that to work.

Don't return `{ success: true }` or `{ success: false }` or similar, in these cases return void or throw an error.

Function parameters should roughly have the structure `params: { ... }, di: {db: SQL, ...}`. Dependencies should be passed in as a separate object. This is a simple way to perform dependency injection without any complicated frameworks.

Enums should be ALL_CAPS, also in the DB.

# Simplicity
Simple is usually better. Don't overcomplicate things. Complexity means bugs and we want reliable code.
If a approach to is changed, remove the code for the old approach.

Only write useful comments. Comments which just repeat the code should be removed.
```ts
const doesTheThing = (params, di) => {
  // do step one <--- this is a redundant comment, remove these
  doStepOne();
  doStepTwo();
  doStepThree();
  return;
}
```

If you find unused code for the task at hand, simply remove it.
If you find unused code that isn't for the task at hand, make a note and ask to remove it after you've completed your task, the user will usually agree.

# Testing - Limited TDD
When writing entirely new features we need to first figure out what it should look like. When we're happy with the shape, then write the test cases.
*In all other cases* such as fixing a bug, *first* write the tests first and then the code. 
First try to reproduce the bug in a test case, and then fix it.

# Database
Don't use offset for pagination, use a cursor instead.
As mentioned in the Types section, ENUMS should be ALL_CAPS.

# UI
Most if not all pages should have their own URL.

# Error Handling
Error messages should be machine readable/constants with a human readable hint. Even though the hint is human readable, the UI should still map the machine readable error code to a human readable string on its own again, so that it can be contextualised and relevant to where the error occurred in the product.

For example: 
A `USER_NOT_FOUND` error code means different things in different contexts. 
At login it means the email or password was incorrect and so the message should be something along the lines of "Invalid email or password".
When adding a user to a project it might mean the email address has a typo or the user might not have an account yet. And so the message should be something along the lines of "User not found. Is the email correct? Is the user registered?".

